# ECG Optimization Project — Phase 2 Results Summary

## Comparison Table

| ID | Technique | Accuracy | Macro F1 | Size (MB) | Speed (ms/sample) |
|:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|
| B0 | Baseline (FP32) | 96.93% | 0.8563 | 0.257 | 0.0150 |
| P1 | Unstructured Pruning (50%) | 97.60% | 0.8798 | 0.942 | 0.0152 |
| P2 | Structured Pruning (30%) | 97.32% | 0.8617 | 0.133 | 0.0276 |
| P3 | Global Magnitude Pruning (40%) | 97.78% | 0.8865 | 0.949 | 0.0280 |
| Q1 | Dynamic Quantization | 96.92% | 0.8560 | 0.234 | 0.5225 |
| Q2 | Static PTQ | 96.94% | 0.8567 | 0.236 | 0.7766 |
| Q3 | Quantization-Aware Training | 97.02% | 0.8603 | 0.236 | 0.5719 |
| Q4 | Weight-only Quantization (FP16) | 96.91% | 0.8553 | 0.138 | 0.2724 |
| Q5 | Mixed Precision (FP16+FP32) | 96.91% | 0.8553 | 0.155 | 0.1888 |

---

## Technical Insights

### 1. Pruning Performance
- **Structured Pruning (P2)** is the clear winner for physical deployment. It reduced the model size by ~48% (from 0.257MB to 0.133MB) while actually **increasing** accuracy to 97.32%.
- **Unstructured (P1)** and **Global (P3)** achieved the highest overall accuracies (97.6% and 97.8%) but resulted in larger file sizes when stored in sparse format. This is due to the overhead of indices in PyTorch's `to_sparse()` for very small parameters counts (~60K params).

### 2. Quantization Performance
- **Weight-only (Q4)** and **Mixed Precision (Q5)** offered significant size reductions with negligible accuracy loss.
- **Integer Quantization (Q1-Q3)** resulted in significantly slower inference on this platform (Mac ARM CPU). This is a known phenomenon for small models where the overhead of quantization logic exceeds the compute benefits.

### 3. Recommendations
For the provided `ECGNet1D` architecture on embedded/IoT hardware:
- **Use P2 (Structured Pruning)** if hardware-agnostic speed and size are priority.
- **Use Q5 (Mixed Precision)** if using hardware with FP16 accelerators (like Apple Neural Engine or ARMv8.2).
- **Optimization Plot**: See `results/optimization/optimization_pareto_front.png` for a visual trade-off analysis.

